WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.220
Real configuration often comes from layers.

2
00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:15.800
A program may have built-in defaults, a default configuration file, a user configuration file, environment variables, command-line overrides, runtime settings, and deployment-provided values.

3
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.020
Safe parsing includes deciding which source wins.

4
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.460
Defaults should be safe and visible.

5
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:32.360
A safe default avoids unnecessary exposure, surprising resource access, excessive permissions, or risky behavior when no explicit configuration is provided.

6
00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:37.740
Defaults should be documented so administrators and reviewers know which behavior occurs in the absence of a setting.

7
00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.820
Precedence order must be explicit.

8
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:48.680
If an environment variable overrides a file, or a command-line option overrides deployment configuration, the parser should apply that order consistently.

9
00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:54.380
Hidden precedence can make the running configuration different from what operators believe they supplied.

10
00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.900
Conflicting settings should be handled predictably.

11
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.160
Some conflicts should be rejected.

12
00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.580
Some can be resolved by precedence.

13
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.680
Some may require a warning or migration path.

14
00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:11.300
The parser should not silently combine incompatible values if the result is unclear.

15
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.060
Layering should not hide behavior changes.

16
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:21.040
A later override may change a security-sensitive setting, a resource location, a timeout, or a feature flag.

17
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:29.540
The program should make final effective configuration observable in a safe way, especially during startup diagnostics or troubleshooting.
